WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.639
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday, July 7th and this is not some kinda fancy camera

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:05.720
trick, I am really that pale.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:13.860
So Hank, I'm gonna do a Question Tuesday today.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.820
But instead of answering a lot of questions quickly,

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.580
I'm going to answer a few questions lengthily.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:23.160
But before I do that I have to change
shirts because right now I match the wall.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.240
Movie Magic!

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.200
Was Brotherhood 2.0 initially your idea or was it Hank's?

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:31.410
Well the initial idea was mine but it
has to be said that I have a crazy idea

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:35.980
at least once every 45 minutes. And I
count on Hank to be like "That is a

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.140
terrible idea" and when I pitched him the
idea for Brotherhood 2.0 he was like

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.940
"That is not terrible", so we did it.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.760
What did you do before you became an author?

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.880
For most of my postcollege life, I worked at this wonderful magazine called Booklist.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.080
But I also worked as a hospital chaplin at a children`s hospital.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.920
And I worked at like a Steak `n Shake

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.400
doing the graveyard shift which I do not recommend.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.320
I mean honestly if anyone is thinking of dropping out of college,

00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:00.940
get thee to Steak `n Shake.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.119
What are the best books for aspiring writers to read as inspiration?

00:01:04.119 --> 00:01:07.310
I'm going to have to say my books. In fact I'm
gonna have to say that the best way to

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:11.290
get inspired to write is to buy twenty
or thirty copies of each of my books.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.930
I`m sorry. You know once you finish your book, they want you to transform from an

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:17.320
extraordinarily pale nerd who sits in a
basement and writes all day

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.660
to some kind of like selling machine.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.300
So, I'm trying to practice a little bit.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:23.550
But really all I wanna do is stay home, make videos, eat Peeps and

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:24.840
hang out with Willie.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.750
What's the meanest thing you ever did to Hank?

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:30.460
Hmm,  I think I might've tried to kill him with a hammer once when we were little.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:32.500
Wait or did he try to kill me?

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:33.700
I'm calling mom.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.640
Hey, it's John. Quick question for you.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.720
Ummm

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.440
Did I try to kill Hank with a hammer when I was little?

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.240
It was the other way around.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.880
He tried to kill me!

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.500
Pwned! Uhm, did I ever try to kill Hank in any in any
way at all?

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.540
Yes

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.040
Oh, I did. Shocks.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.720
What did I specifically do to try
to kill him though?

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.759
I tried to smother him. That's not, that's just hugging.

00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:01.000
That's not killing. These are all very
bad behaviors like it's bad to

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.610
uh pour red cough syrup all over the
bed spread.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:08.330
But it's nothing like Hank trying to
murder me with a hammer.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.200
Well I'm sure I was being irritating but he tried to kill me

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.420
with a hammer.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.120
I feel like I'm really getting the short end of the stick here like

00:02:18.129 --> 00:02:22.000
you're like oh well he did try to
murder you with a hammer but there was

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.280
that one time that you spilled red cough syrup on the bed spread and then

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:27.510
gave him a, gave him a big hug

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:31.970
I'm sorry if I tried to smother Hank by
giving him a hug because I love him so

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.690
much and he tried to kill me with a hammer.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:37.180
Okay so there you have it, according to my mom the meanest thing Hank ever did to

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:40.550
me was try to kill me with a hammer and the meanest thing I ever did to him was

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:43.980
spill some red cough syrup on his bed spread and then hug him too hard.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.220
And yet somehow mom seems to be making the case that I was somehow

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.459
the inferior sibling.

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:50.079
How often do you get recognized?

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.080
Does it make you feel happy or slightly awkward?

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.890
I get recognized pretty often if I'm in
a big public space but it doesn't make

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:58.739
me either happy or slightly awkward. It's just kinda nice to meet a Nerdfighter.

00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:01.250
Like the other day I was standing in
line for the Indiana Jones ride at

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.890
Disneyland and someone tapped me on the shoulder and said

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:07.319
"Are you from YouTube?" and then his little
brother who'd never heard of Nerdfighters

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:08.569
looked up at me and said

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:12.749
"Are you wearing a disguise?" and I was like, "Nope, these are real".

00:03:12.749 --> 00:03:15.420
What advice do you have for writers who can't seem to finish their books?

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:18.810
Not much, I mean it took me four years to finish Looking for Alaska.

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.080
So I'm clearly not an expert in the field of finishing.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:22.030
Just keep at it.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.530
Can I have one of the advanced readers copies of Paper Towns?

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.090
Umm, no. Because I don't have any.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.790
In fact, the people who already won advanced readers

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.079
copies of Paper Towns are still waiting for them.

00:03:30.079 --> 00:03:32.539
A situation which I hope to rectify tomorrow.

00:03:32.549 --> 00:03:36.340
However I do have three of these very
beautiful and very rare Paper Towns

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:37.549
discussion guides.

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:41.181
Which I flagrantly stole from my publisher and then like literally like put in my

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.349
shirt and walked out the convention
center.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.959
So if anybody wants one of these which actually will be really helpful and

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:47.400
interesting when you read the book.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.500
Leave me a question in comments that I can answer

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:50.940
when I do one of these next.

00:03:51.969 --> 00:03:53.509
By the way, did you guys like the paperback cover of

00:03:53.509 --> 00:03:54.869
An Abundance of Katherines?

00:03:54.869 --> 00:03:58.589
Coming out on August 8 for a mere three dollars and ninety nine cents.

